WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.232
Two people sign up for a wallet app at the same moment, and both ask for the handle @ana.

2
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:11.278
Their requests reach two different app servers.

3
00:00:11.278 --> 00:00:15.636
Each one checks for a row with that handle, finds none, and inserts one.

4
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:16.699
Both commit.

5
00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:23.365
Two users now own @ana, and money sent to @ana goes to whichever row a query finds first.

6
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:26.824
Each server said yes without hearing from the other.

7
00:00:26.824 --> 00:00:31.696
The rule needs one decider that sees every claim for a handle, one at a time.

8
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:37.259
The simplest decider is one process reading one log in order: the first claim wins.

9
00:00:37.259 --> 00:00:40.681
But then every claim in the system waits in one line.

10
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.642
Claims for different handles never conflict.

11
00:00:43.642 --> 00:00:49.063
So split the log into four partitions by a hash of the handle, with one processor each.

12
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:51.362
@ana hashes to partition 0.

13
00:00:51.362 --> 00:00:55.066
User 17's claim lands there first, at offset 0.

14
00:00:55.066 --> 00:01:01.136
User 43's claim names the same handle, so it lands right behind, at offset 1.

15
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:03.586
The log now fixes their order.

16
00:01:03.586 --> 00:01:05.613
Processor 0 reads offset 0.

17
00:01:05.613 --> 00:01:10.868
The handle is free, so it records it for user 17 and grants the claim.

18
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:12.741
Then it reads offset 1.

19
00:01:12.741 --> 00:01:16.527
The handle is taken, so user 43's claim is refused.

20
00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:18.755
Nobody else writes that table.

21
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:24.657
Claims for other handles, like @bob, are decided in other partitions at the same time.

22
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:25.924
Now the bug.

23
00:01:25.924 --> 00:01:34.736
Route claims by user id instead: user 17's claim lands in partition 2, and user 43's in partition 3.

24
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:41.382
Two processors with two empty tables both say yes, and @ana has two owners again.

25
00:01:41.382 --> 00:01:45.303
The lesson's simulation counted 937 extra owners.

26
00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:49.292
For a uniqueness rule, the partition key is the constraint.

27
00:01:49.292 --> 00:01:53.117
So every claim for one value has to reach the same decider.
